.Dd August 18, 2026
.Dt LOCALE-GEN 8
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm locale-gen
.Nd compile locales into the archive the C library reads
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar locale ...
.Nm
.Fl -list
.Nm
.Fl -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
compiles the locales
.Xr locale.gen 5
enables into
.Pa /usr/lib/locale/locale-archive ,
which is the file
.Xr setlocale 3
finds a locale in.
Until a locale has been compiled it does not exist as far as any program on the
system is concerned, however completely
.Pa /usr/share/i18n
describes it.
.Pp
With no arguments the whole enabled set is compiled.
With locale names, each is enabled in
.Pa /etc/locale.gen
first and the whole enabled set is then compiled:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
locale-gen pl_PL.UTF-8 de_DE.UTF-8
.Ed
.Pp
A name has to be one glibc supports.
The catalogue is
.Pa /etc/locale.gen
itself, which lists every one of them with the ones this machine is not using
commented out, so
.Ql grep -i portuguese /etc/locale.gen
is how to find a name and
.Ql locale -a
is how to see what has already been compiled.
.Pp
The archive is rebuilt from nothing on every run rather than added to, so
removing a line from
.Pa /etc/locale.gen
and running
.Nm
removes that locale from the system.
It is written to a temporary file and renamed into place, so a run that fails
part way through leaves the locales that were working still working.
.Pp
Nothing about this needs a network or a package.
The locale definitions under
.Pa /usr/share/i18n/locales ,
the character maps beside them and
.Xr localedef 1
are all in the image; they are about 17 MB that the shipped archive uses barely
a fifth of a percent of, which is the point of shipping them.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--list"
.It Fl -list
Print the enabled set, one
.Ql name charset
pair per line, and compile nothing.
.It Fl -help
Print a usage summary.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/usr/lib/locale/locale-archive"
.It Pa /etc/locale.gen
Which locales this machine compiles, and the catalogue of every one it could.
.It Pa /etc/locale.conf
Which of them a login shell starts in.
.Nm
warns when this names a locale the enabled set does not contain, because the
result is otherwise a setlocale warning from every command with nothing to say
which file is wrong.
.It Pa /usr/lib/locale/locale-archive
What is written.
.It Pa /usr/share/i18n/locales
The definitions locales are compiled from.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Add a locale and make it the system's:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
locale-gen fr_FR.UTF-8
printf 'LANG=fr_FR.UTF-8\en' > /etc/locale.conf
.Ed
.Pp
The new locale reaches a shell at its next login.
For the shell that is running:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
export LANG=fr_FR.UTF-8
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr locale 1 ,
.Xr localedef 1 ,
.Xr locale.conf 5 ,
.Xr locale.gen 5
.Pp
.Pa /root/sowa-howto/locale.txt
.Sh AUTHORS
Part of
.Nm sowa ,
whose locale data is glibc's.
.Sh CAVEATS
Only root can run it: it writes
.Pa /usr/lib/locale
and edits
.Pa /etc/locale.gen .
